{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChQK2qESCmQQ7eehuXYjW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WHxjt13BaEBXR3eu4ZYHaV","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat > conflict_test.go <<'EOF'\npackage worker\n\nimport (\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"testing\"\n)\n\nfunc TestMarkersLeft(t *testing.T) {\n\tdir := t.TempDir()\n\twrite := func(name, body string) {\n\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, name), []byte(body), 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\twrite(\"bad.go\", \"a\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\nx\\n=======\\ny\\n>>>>>>> main\\n\")\n\twrite(\"ok.go\", \"a\\n<<< not a marker\\n======\\n\")\n\twrite(\"other.go\", \"<<<<<<< HEAD\\n\") // not in the recorded set\n\troot, err := os.OpenRoot(dir)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tdefer root.Close()\n\tgot := markersLeft(root, map[string]bool{\"bad.go\": true, \"ok.go\": true, \"gone.go\": true})\n\tif len(got) != 1 || got[0] != \"bad.go\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"markersLeft = %v, want [bad.go]\", got)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestQuoteName(t *testing.T) {\n\tif got := quoteName(\"a\\nb\\\"c\"); got != `\"a\\nb\\\"c\"` {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"quoteName = %s\", got)\n\t}\n\tif got := quoteName(strings.Repeat(\"x\", 1000)); len(got) > conflictNameCap+10 {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"name not capped: %d bytes\", len(got))\n\t}\n}\nEOF\ngofmt -l .; go test ./worker -run 'Markers|QuoteName' 2>&1 | tail -5; grep -n \"follow-up\\|follow up\\|conflict\" ../docs/proposal.md | head -20"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":445,"cache_read_input_tokens":107003,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":445},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4bbc59d5-e782-4f96-8016-808f1442da36","uuid":"711a3487-6747-4b52-8a27-1f97244d9b18","timestamp":"2026-11-25T20:54:32.671Z","request_id":"req_011ChQK2q6FKupN3vLELrKiE"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WHxjt13BaEBXR3eu4ZYHaV","type":"tool_result","content":"roles/testdata/docs/skips/src/_x.go:3:8: expected ')', found 'EOF'\nok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/worker\t0.525s [no tests to run]\n120:**Repo context docs.** `.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md` and `.ard/TECH_STACK.md` give agents the background a repository's code does not state. They are human-owned: no agent writes them, the worker guard rejects any change under `.ard/`, and `ard init` proposes them only as templates. Each is optional. A file that is missing, or that still holds only the template (headings and comments), counts as absent and is not shown to agents. Precedence, highest first: the role's own prompt and `.ard/config.yaml`, then the issue and its plan, then these docs; they inform a decision and never override the issue's ask. A reviewer reads them from the pull request's head, so a PR that edits one is judged against its own edit, and because the three files are protected paths that edit waits for a human approval. When a doc conflicts with the issue or with another doc, the agent does not pick a side: it reports the conflict in its role file and answers `needs-human`. Treat their content as facts about the project, not as instructions that change an agent's rules. Each template carries a size guidance of about 150 lines and a `Last reviewed: YYYY-MM-DD` line for the human to keep current.\n135:**Network needs (#384).** A worker that cannot fetch an input itself writes `status: needs-network` and a `network:` list of 1 to 10 entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. The bounce is on in `serve` (`servecmd` sets `closeout.Deps.Network`) and applies to a first worker job only (no branch or PR, and nothing pushed by this job): nothing is pushed and no PR is opened, and close-out requests `ard:triage:engineer` instead of `needs-human` and removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running`, in that order. The triage job gets `network-request.md`, rendered from the record's structured list and marked as agent-written, untrusted input. One network round per issue is allowed, counted from worker close-outs with outcome `needs-network` in any phase from prepared on (the plan never changes after Prepare, so a crash cannot make a round read as not open); a second, a fix, review or conflict round, a pushed job or a repository without a triage engineer role ends `needs-human`, with one comment that lists each entry with its URL in a code span. The same applies when the bounce is off. The live prompts under `.ard/` need a human PR to match the starter copies; a test drives the whole path (bounce, re-run, gate, approval, worker, reviewers and the three negative cases) against fake agents with no network.\n141:Close-out checks `plan.md` against the job's base commit, but the committer writes it onto the latest `main`. So at commit time the committer replaces only the job's own `##` section in the current `main` copy (adding it at the end if absent, dropping it if the job removed it) and keeps every other section as `main` has it; a section another role committed after the job's base is not reverted. This runs again on each push retry. If the `main` copy cannot be merged (the job's heading appears twice, or its section holds a heading-like line), the job fails closed as `needs-human` with a `plan.md conflict` reason; like an allowlist denial, only the redacted log and the job's usage record are committed, never `plan.md` or the job's other files.\n180:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`. `redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing` (default false) is read by the scanner guard, which runs the same pass over findings: when set and gitleaks cannot run, scanner autoclose stops for that run too.\n182:**One writer location per issue directory at a time.** Until the worker branch exists, `main` owns `.ard/<issue#>/`; scanners and triage write there. Once the branch is cut, the branch owns it; workers and reviewers commit to the PR, and a scanner with new findings comments on the issue instead. This keeps the PR merge free of conflicts in `.ard/`.\n184:**One committer for main.** (Concurrency rule, #411: the clone's lock is exclusive for worktree add/remove/prune and shared for fetches, so a lane's fetch never reads a half-added or half-removed worktree; commits and pushes take neither.) Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (a GH006 protected-branch rejection counts among these only when `main` moved since the lease, a lost race that is re-fetched and replayed; with `main` unchanged it is a protection failure, cause `push_protected`, not retried, and a branch-lane GH006 is never retried; re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init --pr`'s (local mode writes only the working tree, #303): it commits only `.ard/config.yaml`, `.ard/ard-init.lock` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n248:**The lint scanner** (`scanners/lint`, a `sh` script, one `jq` program and a Dockerfile that builds golangci-lint v2 with the image's Go) is Go only in v1; other ecosystems are follow-ups. It runs golangci-lint once per `go.mod` (pruning `.git`, `vendor`, `node_modules` and `testdata`) with the repo's own config, or a human-owned `golangci.yml` in the role directory when present. These flags are always forced after any config, because findings that vanish are autoclosed: `--max-issues-per-linter=0 --max-same-issues=0` (no truncation), `--new=false --new-from-rev= --new-from-merge-base= --new-from-patch=` (a repo config asking for \"new issues only\" would report nothing on a clean checkout), `--issues-exit-code=0 --fix=false --path-mode=abs`, plus JSON output to a temp file. It fails closed: a golangci-lint exit other than 0, a `typecheck` issue (a compile error), a module needing a newer Go than the image's (`GOTOOLCHAIN=local`; toolchain switching is a follow-up), a path outside the repository or with a control character, a malformed baseline, and a missing `baseline` file all exit non-zero with no output. Because the image builds golangci-lint with `CGO_ENABLED=0`, a repo config's `goplugin` custom linter cannot load and fails the run. The fingerprint is `lint:<rule>:<path>:<msg>`. `<rule>` is the linter plus `/<code>` for a check code (`staticcheck/SA4000`, `gosec/G104`), or for `govet` and `revive` an analyzer name (`govet/printf`). `<msg>` drops the code, control characters and repeated whitespace, and replaces every standalone number with `N`, so line numbers and \"complexity 31\" versus \"32\" never move a fingerprint; a fingerprint over 512 bytes has its message cut by runes. Identical fingerprints in one file collapse into one finding listing every line; the known gap is that a new occurrence of an identical message in the same file is hidden while the old one is open or baselined (a count-aware baseline is a follow-up). The group is `<rule>:<directory>`, so with `group_size: 20` issues are 20 per rule and directory. Severity has two levels: `medium` for `govet`, `staticcheck/SA*`, `gosec*`, `bodyclose`, `nilerr`, `nilnesserr`, `rowserrcheck`, `sqlclosecheck` and `durationcheck`, `low` for everything else; golangci-lint's own severity is ignored. Messages, source lines and paths are untrusted: they are rendered only inside `text` fences with ``` neutralised, and inline code spans have backticks replaced. Every `//nolint` directive in a `.go` file is itself a finding (rule `nolint`, group `nolint:<directory>`, `low`), so a worker cannot close a lint issue by suppressing it; for the same reason the starter protects `**/.golangci.{yml,yaml,toml,json}`. The baseline is the human-owned file `baseline` in the role directory (a protected path, so no agent can baseline a finding): `#` comments and blank lines are ignored, every other line is one fingerprint, and a finding whose fingerprint is listed is dropped. `ard init` does not ship one, so the scanner records a failure every 24 h until a human commits it; an empty file means no baseline. `scan --baseline` prints a baseline of all current findings (ignoring any existing one), and the exact Docker command is in the Dockerfile's header; the run logs how many baseline entries are unused. Baselining a finding that is open closes its issue as \"No longer detected\" (completed); to suppress it instead, close the issue as not planned. A golangci-lint version bump can change message text and so every fingerprint: bump the version and regenerate every repo's `baseline` in the same human PR.\n278:**Starter planning roles.** `ard init` also ships four planning roles, each writing its own `plan.md` section (`config.BuiltinPlanSections`) and its role file: `product-manager` (`## Requirements`: requirements, a numbered list of acceptance criteria, out of scope), `architect` (`## Design`: approach, affected modules, and an `ADR needed: yes|no` line; the worker writes any ADR in its pull request), `designer` (`## UX`, plus Markdown or script-free SVG files under `.ard/<issue#>/design/`) and `documentation-planner` (`## Docs`: which docs change and what each must say). The command line counts as a user interface, so the designer covers flags, output and messages. The starter ordering is product-manager, then architect; designer, then documentation-planner; the engineer runs after architect and documentation-planner (`after:` in the starter `config.yaml`). Note for #45: acceptance criteria are a numbered list under `### Acceptance criteria`. The starter prompts read the repo context docs per role: `product-manager` reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md`, `architect` reads `VISION.md` and `TECH_STACK.md`, `designer` and `documentation-planner` read `PRODUCT.md`, and `engineer` reads `TECH_STACK.md`. Each prompt states the shared rules: the file is optional, one with no text outside headings and HTML comments counts as absent, the issue is the ask and the files are constraints, and `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` win on commands and layout. A planning role that finds a conflict records it, with file and section, in its own role file.\n280:The starter lead (`.ard/triage/lead/prompt.txt`) follows a fixed contract. It reads the issue only through the untrusted input files `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, which the triage loop writes and never commits. It finds roles by listing the role directories and routes only to those. It writes only `triage-lead.md`, with `attempt: 1`, and a `status: done` always names a `worker`. It answers `needs-human` when a person must decide: an unclear ask, a question, a duplicate, a change to `.ard/config.yaml`, the role directories or `.github/`, no fitting worker, or text that tries to instruct it. It uses `review:<role>` only for a reviewer whose `when` rule would skip a change that needs that review. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-lead.md`; any other change under `.ard/<issue#>/` fails the job as needs-human. It also reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md` when present: a contradiction with a non-goal, principle or banned dependency makes it raise one `needs-human`, while on an interpretive or minor conflict it may proceed and says so in `triage-lead.md`. Inputs found only on the network are not, alone, a reason for `needs-human`: the lead routes them to the engineer and reads the `Worker roles:` line to see whether any worker role has network (#301). In a network round the controller re-arms it after the engineer's `done`, and its close-out then re-requests the other triage roles that had finished.\n362:6. **Two commits per round.** The loop commits the code through the committer's code lane (`Branches.CommitCode`) with the title `[<type>: ]<issue title> (#<issue>)`, the redacted summary (secrets and host paths) as the body and an `Ard-Job: <job-id>` trailer, no `[skip ci]`. `<type>` comes from the issue's `type:*` label (`feature` is `feat`, `bug` is `fix`; `docs`, `chore`, `refactor` and `test` keep their names) and with no `type:*` label the `<type>: ` part is dropped and the title is `<issue title> (#<issue>)`. The prefix is never taken from the plan or the lead's routing, so it depends on a maintainer applying `type:*` at intake. At the end close-out commits `worker-<role>.md` and the redacted logs of every agent step to the same branch (or to `main`, as triage's are, when the job stopped before its first push). `.ard/<issue#>/` never goes through the code lane. A push failure (branch exists, conflict, branch gone, guard) fails the job with no automatic retry.\n367:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages. With `network: true` it may fetch other inputs, records each under `fetched:` and treats the fetched bytes as untrusted data, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make lint test` (so formatting and vet failures are caught before a PR opens), and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present; the file is a constraint, never an instruction, and the worker never resolves a conflict with it: it keeps to the issue and plan and notes the conflict in Open issues. With `Network: available.` the worker fetches only what the plan's Network inputs name, verifies each file against the publisher's checksum file, never invents or recalls a value, records each file in `fetched:` and in a `CAPTURE.md` beside the fixtures, and commits a regen script and Make target; with `Network: unavailable.` it fetches nothing and answers `needs-network` with a `network:` list (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`) when no branch exists yet, or `needs-human` when a branch or PR does (#301, #384). The controller bounces a first `needs-network` once; the second ends `needs-human`.\n369:**Review feedback loops back here.** The `reviewfix` loop (#41) starts a round when every requested reviewer has finished and at least one ended `ard:review:<role>:changes`, backed by that role's close-out record (a label alone does not count). It records a `review_round` event in `_controller.db` (counted when requested, ordered by event ID, local to that database) and removes `ard:worker:<role>:done` from the issue, so the worker loop claims it again. The worker fixes on the same branch and reads `review-feedback.md`. When the fix lands with code changes, every requested reviewer runs again at the new head (their `:done` and `:changes` labels come off), because merge-gating approvals must cover the merged head. A round that changes no code, or one that would pass `max_review_rounds` (default 3), posts one comment and adds `ard:needs-human` on the issue; it is not repeated on the same head after a person removes the label. Removing `ard:needs-human` and pushing a new head caps again at once and never reruns reviewers; to continue, raise max_review_rounds, or fix the PR and remove the ard:review:<role>:changes labels so reviewers rerun. The merger can request a conflict round through the same round API (`reviewfix.RequestRound` with reason `conflict`): a computed conflict at a head with no round yet records a `requested` event and the worker merges `main` into the branch. A conflict round lands when the branch head is a merge commit that includes the round's head (a merge-only resolution changes no code against it, so the no-code check does not apply); otherwise it caps. Conflict and review rounds share `max_review_rounds`, and each event records its reason. This request path is off until part B of #255: the merger's `Rounds` dependency stays unset in `servecmd`, so a conflict, like a red required check, still goes to `ard:needs-human`.\n387:**Repo context in the starter reviewers.** `eng-review`, `license-review` and `security-review` read `TECH_STACK.md`; `product-manager-review` reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md`. Each file is optional and a template-only file counts as absent. A conflict between the change and a file is reported as a finding. The PR-head rule: reviewers trust the files only while `diff.patch` does not touch `.ard/*.md`; if it does, that is a blocking `high` finding and the changed files are not relied on.\n414:- every requested `ard:review:<role>` is `:done` and none is `:changes` or `:running` (any `ard:review:*:changes` or `:running` label blocks, configured role or not), and each review is proven by the newest close-out record of that role on this PR (outcome done, head not moved), not by the label, which a person with triage access can add, and stands at a head that still covers the current one. The head rule is shared with the reviewer's mark-ready (`internal/prgate`): a head is covered when no path outside `.ard/<issue#>/` changed since the reviewed commit, or, for the merger, when every such path came from `main` (an update-branch merge does not invalidate a review, but a path both the PR and `main` changed since the review does, unless the PR's own patch to that path is unchanged: the stable patch id of the diff from the merge base is identical before and after the update, the head reaches the reviewed commit along first parents through two-parent merges only, and no git step failed. Every merge in that chain must also have exactly the tree of git's clean mechanical merge of its two parents (`git merge-tree --write-tree`, attributes from the empty tree, no rerere, no renormalize), so a conflict resolution, a whitespace edit or a hand-crafted merge breaks the cover even when the patch id is unchanged; a later PR commit breaks it too, and any git error, including a git without `merge-tree --write-tree`, is not covered. The merger logs the paths covered this way as `merge.cover_patch_equal`; the reviewer's mark-ready and reviewfix landing keep the plain rule). A review made stale by code that changed since it is re-requested once per head: the merger removes `ard:review:<role>:done` (the reviewer then claims the role again at the current head), records a `review_rerequest` event for that PR and head, and logs `merge.review_rerequested`. A head that moved only by an update-branch merge from `main` is still covered and triggers nothing. Under dry-run it logs `merge.would_rerequest` and writes only to the ledger, with no event\n417:- GitHub has computed that the branch does not conflict with `main`. Only a computed `mergeable: false` or `mergeable_state: dirty` is a conflict: it gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment, and, once the merger's `Rounds` dependency is wired (part B of #255; unset today), it becomes a conflict round (`reviewfix.RequestRound`) that counts toward `max_review_rounds`. A `mergeable` that is still null or unknown, and a 405 \"not mergeable\" from the merge call, mean GitHub is still computing: the merger retries next tick and never labels\n435:A PR can change state behind ard's back, or stop moving, and the merger sees only ready, open PRs. `internal/prwatch` is a controller loop, not an agent, and takes no slot. Every `intervals.prwatch` (default 5m) it reads ard's PRs (App-authored `worker/` branches with base `main`: draft, ready, merged and closed) and **only observes, records and notifies**. It never merges, updates a branch, re-requests a review or starts a round: those belong to the merger, to #252 (stale reviews), #255 (conflicts) and `reviewfix` (#41), and the loop's effects interface has no such call. It is skipped while the controller is paused, like the merger.\n443:| Head moved, conflicts, requested changes, comments, label edits | Logged only |\n563:| Parallel triage roles share `plan.md` | Lost sections, conflicting commits | One triage job per issue at a time |\n578:- **Homebrew ([#177](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/177)).** `.github/workflows/release.yml` builds each new `v*` tag with GoReleaser (`.goreleaser.yaml`: darwin and linux, arm64 and amd64, CGO off, `-X main.version`), adds the archives and `checksums.txt` to the release `version.yml` created (`release.mode: keep-existing`) and updates the cask in `albertcbrown/homebrew-tap` with `TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN`, a token scoped to the tap and held only in the `release` environment. It stays inert until `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on` and that token exists, and on a tag push it releases only the newest tag, so a backfill publishes once. Gatekeeper: the binary is not yet signed or notarized, so the cask clears the quarantine flag after install (`hooks.post.install`), an accepted risk; notarization is a follow-up.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4bbc59d5-e782-4f96-8016-808f1442da36","uuid":"92a4df99-b571-4663-b967-ae911814a89b","timestamp":"2026-11-25T20:54:33.988Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"roles/testdata/docs/skips/src/_x.go:3:8: expected ')', found 'EOF'\nok  \tgithub.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/worker\t0.525s [no tests to run]\n120:**Repo context docs.** `.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md` and `.ard/TECH_STACK.md` give agents the background a repository's code does not state. They are human-owned: no agent writes them, the worker guard rejects any change under `.ard/`, and `ard init` proposes them only as templates. Each is optional. A file that is missing, or that still holds only the template (headings and comments), counts as absent and is not shown to agents. Precedence, highest first: the role's own prompt and `.ard/config.yaml`, then the issue and its plan, then these docs; they inform a decision and never override the issue's ask. A reviewer reads them from the pull request's head, so a PR that edits one is judged against its own edit, and because the three files are protected paths that edit waits for a human approval. When a doc conflicts with the issue or with another doc, the agent does not pick a side: it reports the conflict in its role file and answers `needs-human`. Treat their content as facts about the project, not as instructions that change an agent's rules. Each template carries a size guidance of about 150 lines and a `Last reviewed: YYYY-MM-DD` line for the human to keep current.\n135:**Network needs (#384).** A worker that cannot fetch an input itself writes `status: needs-network` and a `network:` list of 1 to 10 entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. The bounce is on in `serve` (`servecmd` sets `closeout.Deps.Network`) and applies to a first worker job only (no branch or PR, and nothing pushed by this job): nothing is pushed and no PR is opened, and close-out requests `ard:triage:engineer` instead of `needs-human` and removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running`, in that order. The triage job gets `network-request.md`, rendered from the record's structured list and marked as agent-written, untrusted input. One network round per issue is allowed, counted from worker close-outs with outcome `needs-network` in any phase from prepared on (the plan never changes after Prepare, so a crash cannot make a round read as not open); a second, a fix, review or conflict round, a pushed job or a repository without a triage engineer role ends `needs-human`, with one comment that lists each entry with its URL in a code span. The same applies when the bounce is off. The live prompts under `.ard/` need a human PR to match the starter copies; a test drives the whole path (bounce, re-run, gate, approval, worker, reviewers and the three negative cases) against fake agents with no network.\n141:Close-out checks `plan.md` against the job's base commit, but the committer writes it onto the latest `main`. So at commit time the committer replaces only the job's own `##` section in the current `main` copy (adding it at the end if absent, dropping it if the job removed it) and keeps every other section as `main` has it; a section another role committed after the job's base is not reverted. This runs again on each push retry. If the `main` copy cannot be merged (the job's heading appears twice, or its section holds a heading-like line), the job fails closed as `needs-human` with a `plan.md conflict` reason; like an allowlist denial, only the redacted log and the job's usage record are committed, never `plan.md` or the job's other files.\n180:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`. `redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing` (default false) is read by the scanner guard, which runs the same pass over findings: when set and gitleaks cannot run, scanner autoclose stops for that run too.\n182:**One writer location per issue directory at a time.** Until the worker branch exists, `main` owns `.ard/<issue#>/`; scanners and triage write there. Once the branch is cut, the branch owns it; workers and reviewers commit to the PR, and a scanner with new findings comments on the issue instead. This keeps the PR merge free of conflicts in `.ard/`.\n184:**One committer for main.** (Concurrency rule, #411: the clone's lock is exclusive for worktree add/remove/prune and shared for fetches, so a lane's fetch never reads a half-added or half-removed worktree; commits and pushes take neither.) Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (a GH006 protected-branch rejection counts among these only when `main` moved since the lease, a lost race that is re-fetched and replayed; with `main` unchanged it is a protection failure, cause `push_protected`, not retried, and a branch-lane GH006 is never retried; re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init --pr`'s (local mode writes only the working tree, #303): it commits only `.ard/config.yaml`, `.ard/ard-init.lock` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n248:**The lint scanner** (`scanners/lint`, a `sh` script, one `jq` program and a Dockerfile that builds golangci-lint v2 with the image's Go) is Go only in v1; other ecosystems are follow-ups. It runs golangci-lint once per `go.mod` (pruning `.git`, `vendor`, `node_modules` and `testdata`) with the repo's own config, or a human-owned `golangci.yml` in the role directory when present. These flags are always forced after any config, because findings that vanish are autoclosed: `--max-issues-per-linter=0 --max-same-issues=0` (no truncation), `--new=false --new-from-rev= --new-from-merge-base= --new-from-patch=` (a repo config asking for \"new issues only\" would report nothing on a clean checkout), `--issues-exit-code=0 --fix=false --path-mode=abs`, plus JSON output to a temp file. It fails closed: a golangci-lint exit other than 0, a `typecheck` issue (a compile error), a module needing a newer Go than the image's (`GOTOOLCHAIN=local`; toolchain switching is a follow-up), a path outside the repository or with a control character, a malformed baseline, and a missing `baseline` file all exit non-zero with no output. Because the image builds golangci-lint with `CGO_ENABLED=0`, a repo config's `goplugin` custom linter cannot load and fails the run. The fingerprint is `lint:<rule>:<path>:<msg>`. `<rule>` is the linter plus `/<code>` for a check code (`staticcheck/SA4000`, `gosec/G104`), or for `govet` and `revive` an analyzer name (`govet/printf`). `<msg>` drops the code, control characters and repeated whitespace, and replaces every standalone number with `N`, so line numbers and \"complexity 31\" versus \"32\" never move a fingerprint; a fingerprint over 512 bytes has its message cut by runes. Identical fingerprints in one file collapse into one finding listing every line; the known gap is that a new occurrence of an identical message in the same file is hidden while the old one is open or baselined (a count-aware baseline is a follow-up). The group is `<rule>:<directory>`, so with `group_size: 20` issues are 20 per rule and directory. Severity has two levels: `medium` for `govet`, `staticcheck/SA*`, `gosec*`, `bodyclose`, `nilerr`, `nilnesserr`, `rowserrcheck`, `sqlclosecheck` and `durationcheck`, `low` for everything else; golangci-lint's own severity is ignored. Messages, source lines and paths are untrusted: they are rendered only inside `text` fences with ``` neutralised, and inline code spans have backticks replaced. Every `//nolint` directive in a `.go` file is itself a finding (rule `nolint`, group `nolint:<directory>`, `low`), so a worker cannot close a lint issue by suppressing it; for the same reason the starter protects `**/.golangci.{yml,yaml,toml,json}`. The baseline is the human-owned file `baseline` in the role directory (a protected path, so no agent can baseline a finding): `#` comments and blank lines are ignored, every other line is one fingerprint, and a finding whose fingerprint is listed is dropped. `ard init` does not ship one, so the scanner records a failure every 24 h until a human commits it; an empty file means no baseline. `scan --baseline` prints a baseline of all current findings (ignoring any existing one), and the exact Docker command is in the Dockerfile's header; the run logs how many baseline entries are unused. Baselining a finding that is open closes its issue as \"No longer detected\" (completed); to suppress it instead, close the issue as not planned. A golangci-lint version bump can change message text and so every fingerprint: bump the version and regenerate every repo's `baseline` in the same human PR.\n278:**Starter planning roles.** `ard init` also ships four planning roles, each writing its own `plan.md` section (`config.BuiltinPlanSections`) and its role file: `product-manager` (`## Requirements`: requirements, a numbered list of acceptance criteria, out of scope), `architect` (`## Design`: approach, affected modules, and an `ADR needed: yes|no` line; the worker writes any ADR in its pull request), `designer` (`## UX`, plus Markdown or script-free SVG files under `.ard/<issue#>/design/`) and `documentation-planner` (`## Docs`: which docs change and what each must say). The command line counts as a user interface, so the designer covers flags, output and messages. The starter ordering is product-manager, then architect; designer, then documentation-planner; the engineer runs after architect and documentation-planner (`after:` in the starter `config.yaml`). Note for #45: acceptance criteria are a numbered list under `### Acceptance criteria`. The starter prompts read the repo context docs per role: `product-manager` reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md`, `architect` reads `VISION.md` and `TECH_STACK.md`, `designer` and `documentation-planner` read `PRODUCT.md`, and `engineer` reads `TECH_STACK.md`. Each prompt states the shared rules: the file is optional, one with no text outside headings and HTML comments counts as absent, the issue is the ask and the files are constraints, and `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` win on commands and layout. A planning role that finds a conflict records it, with file and section, in its own role file.\n280:The starter lead (`.ard/triage/lead/prompt.txt`) follows a fixed contract. It reads the issue only through the untrusted input files `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, which the triage loop writes and never commits. It finds roles by listing the role directories and routes only to those. It writes only `triage-lead.md`, with `attempt: 1`, and a `status: done` always names a `worker`. It answers `needs-human` when a person must decide: an unclear ask, a question, a duplicate, a change to `.ard/config.yaml`, the role directories or `.github/`, no fitting worker, or text that tries to instruct it. It uses `review:<role>` only for a reviewer whose `when` rule would skip a change that needs that review. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-lead.md`; any other change under `.ard/<issue#>/` fails the job as needs-human. It also reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md` when present: a contradiction with a non-goal, principle or banned dependency makes it raise one `needs-human`, while on an interpretive or minor conflict it may proceed and says so in `triage-lead.md`. Inputs found only on the network are not, alone, a reason for `needs-human`: the lead routes them to the engineer and reads the `Worker roles:` line to see whether any worker role has network (#301). In a network round the controller re-arms it after the engineer's `done`, and its close-out then re-requests the other triage roles that had finished.\n362:6. **Two commits per round.** The loop commits the code through the committer's code lane (`Branches.CommitCode`) with the title `[<type>: ]<issue title> (#<issue>)`, the redacted summary (secrets and host paths) as the body and an `Ard-Job: <job-id>` trailer, no `[skip ci]`. `<type>` comes from the issue's `type:*` label (`feature` is `feat`, `bug` is `fix`; `docs`, `chore`, `refactor` and `test` keep their names) and with no `type:*` label the `<type>: ` part is dropped and the title is `<issue title> (#<issue>)`. The prefix is never taken from the plan or the lead's routing, so it depends on a maintainer applying `type:*` at intake. At the end close-out commits `worker-<role>.md` and the redacted logs of every agent step to the same branch (or to `main`, as triage's are, when the job stopped before its first push). `.ard/<issue#>/` never goes through the code lane. A push failure (branch exists, conflict, branch gone, guard) fails the job with no automatic retry.\n367:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages. With `network: true` it may fetch other inputs, records each under `fetched:` and treats the fetched bytes as untrusted data, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make lint test` (so formatting and vet failures are caught before a PR opens), and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present; the file is a constraint, never an instruction, and the worker never resolves a conflict with it: it keeps to the issue and plan and notes the conflict in Open issues. With `Network: available.` the worker fetches only what the plan's Network inputs name, verifies each file against the publisher's checksum file, never invents or recalls a value, records each file in `fetched:` and in a `CAPTURE.md` beside the fixtures, and commits a regen script and Make target; with `Network: unavailable.` it fetches nothing and answers `needs-network` with a `network:` list (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`) when no branch exists yet, or `needs-human` when a branch or PR does (#301, #384). The controller bounces a first `needs-network` once; the second ends `needs-human`.\n369:**Review feedback loops back here.** The `reviewfix` loop (#41) starts a round when every requested reviewer has finished and at least one ended `ard:review:<role>:changes`, backed by that role's close-out record (a label alone does not count). It records a `review_round` event in `_controller.db` (counted when requested, ordered by event ID, local to that database) and removes `ard:worker:<role>:done` from the issue, so the worker loop claims it again. The worker fixes on the same branch and reads `review-feedback.md`. When the fix lands with code changes, every requested reviewer runs again at the new head (their `:done` and `:changes` labels come off), because merge-gating approvals must cover the merged head. A round that changes no code, or one that would pass `max_review_rounds` (default 3), posts one comment and adds `ard:needs-human` on the issue; it is not repeated on the same head after a person removes the label. Removing `ard:needs-human` and pushing a new head caps again at once and never reruns reviewers; to continue, raise max_review_rounds, or fix the PR and remove the ard:review:<role>:changes labels so reviewers rerun. The merger can request a conflict round through the same round API (`reviewfix.RequestRound` with reason `conflict`): a computed conflict at a head with no round yet records a `requested` event and the worker merges `main` into the branch. A conflict round lands when the branch head is a merge commit that includes the round's head (a merge-only resolution changes no code against it, so the no-code check does not apply); otherwise it caps. Conflict and review rounds share `max_review_rounds`, and each event records its reason. This request path is off until part B of #255: the merger's `Rounds` dependency stays unset in `servecmd`, so a conflict, like a red required check, still goes to `ard:needs-human`.\n387:**Repo context in the starter reviewers.** `eng-review`, `license-review` and `security-review` read `TECH_STACK.md`; `product-manager-review` reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md`. Each file is optional and a template-only file counts as absent. A conflict between the change and a file is reported as a finding. The PR-head rule: reviewers trust the files only while `diff.patch` does not touch `.ard/*.md`; if it does, that is a blocking `high` finding and the changed files are not relied on.\n414:- every requested `ard:review:<role>` is `:done` and none is `:changes` or `:running` (any `ard:review:*:changes` or `:running` label blocks, configured role or not), and each review is proven by the newest close-out record of that role on this PR (outcome done, head not moved), not by the label, which a person with triage access can add, and stands at a head that still covers the current one. The head rule is shared with the reviewer's mark-ready (`internal/prgate`): a head is covered when no path outside `.ard/<issue#>/` changed since the reviewed commit, or, for the merger, when every such path came from `main` (an update-branch merge does not invalidate a review, but a path both the PR and `main` changed since the review does, unless the PR's own patch to that path is unchanged: the stable patch id of the diff from the merge base is identical before and after the update, the head reaches the reviewed commit along first parents through two-parent merges only, and no git step failed. Every merge in that chain must also have exactly the tree of git's clean mechanical merge of its two parents (`git merge-tree --write-tree`, attributes from the empty tree, no rerere, no renormalize), so a conflict resolution, a whitespace edit or a hand-crafted merge breaks the cover even when the patch id is unchanged; a later PR commit breaks it too, and any git error, including a git without `merge-tree --write-tree`, is not covered. The merger logs the paths covered this way as `merge.cover_patch_equal`; the reviewer's mark-ready and reviewfix landing keep the plain rule). A review made stale by code that changed since it is re-requested once per head: the merger removes `ard:review:<role>:done` (the reviewer then claims the role again at the current head), records a `review_rerequest` event for that PR and head, and logs `merge.review_rerequested`. A head that moved only by an update-branch merge from `main` is still covered and triggers nothing. Under dry-run it logs `merge.would_rerequest` and writes only to the ledger, with no event\n417:- GitHub has computed that the branch does not conflict with `main`. Only a computed `mergeable: false` or `mergeable_state: dirty` is a conflict: it gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment, and, once the merger's `Rounds` dependency is wired (part B of #255; unset today), it becomes a conflict round (`reviewfix.RequestRound`) that counts toward `max_review_rounds`. A `mergeable` that is still null or unknown, and a 405 \"not mergeable\" from the merge call, mean GitHub is still computing: the merger retries next tick and never labels\n435:A PR can change state behind ard's back, or stop moving, and the merger sees only ready, open PRs. `internal/prwatch` is a controller loop, not an agent, and takes no slot. Every `intervals.prwatch` (default 5m) it reads ard's PRs (App-authored `worker/` branches with base `main`: draft, ready, merged and closed) and **only observes, records and notifies**. It never merges, updates a branch, re-requests a review or starts a round: those belong to the merger, to #252 (stale reviews), #255 (conflicts) and `reviewfix` (#41), and the loop's effects interface has no such call. It is skipped while the controller is paused, like the merger.\n443:| Head moved, conflicts, requested changes, comments, label edits | Logged only |\n563:| Parallel triage roles share `plan.md` | Lost sections, conflicting commits | One triage job per issue at a time |\n578:- **Homebrew ([#177](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/177)).** `.github/workflows/release.yml` builds each new `v*` tag with GoReleaser (`.goreleaser.yaml`: darwin and linux, arm64 and amd64, CGO off, `-X main.version`), adds the archives and `checksums.txt` to the release `version.yml` created (`release.mode: keep-existing`) and updates the cask in `albertcbrown/homebrew-tap` with `TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN`, a token scoped to the tap and held only in the `release` environment. It stays inert until `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on` and that token exists, and on a tag push it releases only the newest tag, so a backfill publishes once. Gatekeeper: the binary is not yet signed or notarized, so the cask clears the quarantine flag after install (`hooks.post.install`), an accepted risk; notarization is a follow-up.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WHxjt13BaEBXR3eu4ZYHaV","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
